<Project>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net48'">
    <!-- Building a net48 target off Windows needs the .NET Framework reference assemblies
         as a package, and the SDK adds this reference implicitly — but only there. It then
         lands in packages.lock.json, and CI restores in locked mode on Windows, where the
         implicit reference does not exist: NU1004, naming a package nobody wrote down, on a
         machine that never ran the build that changed the file (GH-73).

         Declaring it makes both platforms resolve the same graph. PrivateAssets="all" keeps
         it out of the published packages' dependencies, since it is purely a build-time
         concern for the compiler's reference assemblies.

         In .targets rather than .props because $(TargetFramework) is not yet set when
         Directory.Build.props is evaluated for a single-target project. The condition is
         per-TargetFramework, so it also applies to the net48 half of the multi-targeted
         projects and not to their netstandard2.0 or net8.0 halves. The old-style VB samples
         use TargetFrameworkVersion and the installed targeting pack, so they neither match
         this nor need it. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
